<Text
  title="Privacy"
  heading="What we know about you: nothing."
  description="How the écho website and the écho app handle your data: no analytics, no cookies, no third parties on the site; an encrypted index that stays on your Mac in the app."
>
  <p>écho is about keeping your mail and messages to yourself, so this website and the app are built the same way. Here is what that means in practice.</p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No analytics, no tracking.</strong> We don’t count visitors, record clicks or build profiles.</li>
    <li><strong>No cookies.</strong> The site doesn’t set any, and doesn’t use local storage either.</li>
    <li><strong>No third parties.</strong> Fonts, icons and images are served from echo.kimiyu.ch itself. There are no embeds, no ad networks and no scripts from anyone else, so your browser only ever talks to this one server.</li>
    <li><strong>Server logs.</strong> The site is hosted by Hostpoint in Switzerland. Like any web server, theirs keeps standard access logs (IP address, time, the page requested, browser) for operation and security, for a limited time. We don’t use them to identify anyone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If you write to us, we use your email only to answer you.</p>

  <h2>The app</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your index stays on your Mac.</strong> écho reads your mail, chats, files, calendar, notes, reminders and contacts where they already are and keeps its search index on your disk, encrypted, with the key in your Keychain. Settings → Privacy shows how big the index is and where it’s stored.</li>
    <li><strong>App lock.</strong> Lock écho with Touch ID or your password: at launch, after a few minutes idle, when the screen locks, or with ⌃⌘L. It keeps indexing while it’s locked.</li>
    <li><strong>Changes only what you ask.</strong> écho reads your sources and leaves them as they are, with two exceptions you make yourself. Archive or delete mail from écho and it does that in Apple Mail (macOS asks you to allow it the first time, and there’s Undo). Drop a link on écho and it saves it as a .webloc file in a folder you pick. écho never sends anything.</li>
    <li><strong>No account, no server.</strong> There is nothing to sign up for and nothing to sync. écho itself doesn’t upload your data anywhere. The one exception is an AI assistant you connect yourself (see below).</li>
    <li><strong>Dropped links.</strong> When you drop a link on écho, it makes one request to that page to read its title and description. No cookies, no cache, and nothing about you is sent. Links already in your folders are never fetched. Switch it off in Sources → Files (“Fetch title and description when you drop a link”).</li>
    <li><strong>Updates only if you say so.</strong> Checking for updates is off until you turn it on in Settings → General → Updates, or choose Check for Updates… in the app menu. A check asks echo.kimiyu.ch once for the list of versions; it sends the app’s name and version and nothing about you or your data. Updates are signed, and écho installs only ones signed by us.</li>
    <li><strong>You choose the sources.</strong> Pick the mailboxes and folders it reads, pause them or remove them at any time. Switch a whole source off and écho stops reading it and removes its items from the index (it asks first); switch it on and it reads it all again. Leave out single folders, chats or senders, and écho removes what it already read from them.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete everything.</strong> Settings → Privacy → Delete all data… asks for Touch ID or your password, deletes the index and its key, and quits écho. Your mail, chats and files themselves are not touched.</li>
    <li><strong>Assistants only if you say so.</strong> Connecting an AI assistant over MCP (Claude Desktop, Claude Code or another MCP client) is off until you switch it on. The assistant only sees the sources you allow, never what you left out, and nothing while écho is locked. It can only read. It sees names, not email addresses or phone numbers, unless you allow them in Settings → Agents. What it reads from your mail, chats and files is marked as written by other people, so your assistant reports it and doesn’t follow instructions hidden in it. Every access is logged in écho: when, which assistant, what it searched for or opened, never the content. What it reads leaves your Mac: it goes to that assistant’s provider, under that provider’s terms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>Write to <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a>.</p>
</Text>
